> No worries about L's ?

Absolutely no. In fact, when I do start genociding (quite rare
occurrence, in truth), I most emphatically make certain that I don't
genocide any liches. Well, make that around. I make certain that I do
genocide arch liches, but I make certain that the scroll is cursed
before doing so.

In case you need to toughen up quite quickly (in real time), nothing
beats obtaining two cursed scrolls of genocide and a wand of fire. Go
to some branch end (last level of Quest is my favorite, mine bottom
and Castle work reasonably well, as does Fort Ludious if you have the
Eye of Mispelling). There, go to the upstairs and burn E there. Next,
take a step up, go in the middle of a large room and read first scroll
to reverse genocide cockatrices. Kill one, get the corpse, and come
back. Next, stand on the stairs and reverse genocide some arch liches,
and bash all monsters they summon with the corpse.

Repeat this a couple of times, you'll get on average some 6-10 potions
of gain level, 10-15 full healing, and about 20 extra healing waiting
for you inside the statues.

So, the black dragon genocide is for protecting the liches that do the
summoning, as is the umber hulk one. Genociding stoning-resistant
monsters increases the yield since less time is wasted slowly bashing
those to death.

Doing this is in my opinion bordering an abuse, but it is great fun in
moderation. Even with a burned "E" it is far from safe, though, and I
think that I've lost more post-Quest characters doing this than for
any other reason. This is because after a while cockatrice corpses
start to accumulate to the level for various reasons (mainly they get
caught by dragon breaths), and captains have a nasty habit of picking
them up and making a statue out of me. Or someone digs a pit and I
don't notice it and fall in with a cockatrice corpse. Or something. I
think I've died perhaps five or six times like that (+ a number of
wasted amulets of life saving), each time promising myself that next
time I'll be more careful.

